[Music]  police in Florida arrested a possible  serial killer using genetic genealogy  technology and DNA collected from a  cigarette Robert Hayes was charged in  Palm Beach County with first-degree  murder for the death of one woman but  more charges are expected in Daytona  Beach police say the 37 year old is  believed to have killed at least four  more women over ten years  Manuel Bojorquez is following this story  Robert Hayes was denied bail Monday when  he appeared in court in Palm Beach  County we hadn't put this individual in  jail he would have done this again and  we would have had another innocent  victim out here according to police  Hayes was a college student at  bethune-cookman university in Daytona  Beach studying criminal justice in 2005  and 2006 when he is believed to have  shot and killed three women  laQuita Gunther Julie Greene and Awana  Patton but on Monday Hayes was charged  for a more recent murder in 2016 Rachel  Elizabeth Bay was found dead along a  road in Palm Beach County Florida State  Attorney RJ Larissa it's not just  working the streets anymore it's working  with DNA it's working with technology  according to an arrest affidavit it was  DNA recently found on a discarded  cigarette and genetic geneology  technology made popular with companies  like 23andme and ancestry.com that  linked Hayes to the Palm Beach County  murder and then to two of the Daytona  murders police say ballistic evidence  connected him to a third murder in  Daytona and he could be connected to a  fourth Daytona police say they continued  to build their case before charging  Hayes Volusia County Sheriff Michael  Chitwood said he spoke to one of the  victims families they are absolutely  estatic they didn't think they'd be  alive to see this day come the Palm  Beach County public defender's office  which represents Hayes has not yet  responded to our request for comment for  CBS this morning Manuel Bojorquez Miami

